WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.100 align:start position:0%
This isn't a threat. It's a stream of
50,000 molten tin droplets being shot

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:11.400 align:start position:0%
out every second. And without this tiny
stream, the trillion-dollar tech

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.200 align:start position:0%
industry would grind to a halt. When you
think about the biggest tech companies

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:19.100 align:start position:0%
in the world, you probably think about
Google, Amazon, or Microsoft. But they

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.100 align:start position:0%
run on massive data centers built on
computer chips. And those chips have to

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:29.000 align:start position:0%
be designed by companies like NVIDIA.
But they don't actually make the chips.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:33.100 align:start position:0%
Another company like TSMC usually makes
them. But if they want the most advanced

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.100 align:start position:0%
chips, they need the machines from one
company, ASML.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.600 align:start position:0%
These machines are the most complicated
consumer product humanity has ever

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.800 align:start position:0%
built. Each droplet in that stream is
hit three times in a row to create a

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.800 align:start position:0%
plasma that's 40 times hotter than the
surface of the sun. Then the light

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000 align:start position:0%
emitted from the plasma bounces off
mirrors that just might be the smoothest

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:58.600 align:start position:0%
objects in the universe. If they were
the size of the Earth, the tallest bump

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:01.800 align:start position:0%
would be no bigger than a playing card.
That light makes the most critical

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:06.500 align:start position:0%
layers of advanced chips, those that
have features only 8 nanometers wide.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:10.900 align:start position:0%
That's roughly 1,000 times smaller than
a red blood cell. And every single line

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:14.800 align:start position:0%
has to be perfectly placed. Here's how
accurate this machine is. If you place

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:19.100 align:start position:0%
the laser on that final mirror, point it
to a dime on the moon, you could control

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.400 align:start position:0%
the tilt of the mirror so the laser move
from one side of the dime to the other.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.400 align:start position:0%
And that's all happening while the wafer
is whipping back and forth on the fly,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.900 align:start position:0%
adjusting its height so that every
single chip layer is perfect at most

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.400 align:start position:0%
five silicon atoms off. If you want to
know more about this insane machine and

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:41.100 align:start position:0%
why this was actually the easiest
solution to make the next generation of

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.100 align:start position:0%
chips, watch our full video on YouTube.

